<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Why is my gum swelling near a specific tooth?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Gum swelling near one tooth is almost always caused by either a dental abscess (infection inside the tooth spreading to the root tip) or a periodontal abscess (infection in a gum pocket). Both produce a painful gumboil. Our X-ray examination at CARE Hospitals determines which is present and what treatment is needed.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Can a gumboil heal on its own?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rowate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">No — a gumboil represents an active bacterial infection. It may temporarily reduce in size if the pus drains into the mouth, but the source tooth infection remains. Without treatment of the source (root canal or extraction), the gumboil recurs. Antibiotics alone without addressing the cause are not a cure.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Will I need my tooth removed for a gumboil?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Not necessarily — if the tooth is structurally sound and there is sufficient bone support, root canal treatment can eliminate the infection and save the tooth. Extraction is reserved for non-restorable teeth or teeth with severe periodontal bone loss. Our surgeons always assess whether a tooth can be saved first.</div>
</details>
</div>
